    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "Hey, I'm working on a deck build in Vancouver. Poured concrete footings are set, but I need to double check my math for the joist spacing and beam sizing. Can you explain the difference between live load and dead load in the context of deck design and how I should be accounting for them in my calculations? I'm using western red cedar for the framing.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "In deck design, live load refers to the weight of people, furniture, and other objects that will be placed on the deck, typically 150-200 pounds per square foot. Dead load, on the other hand, refers to the weight of the deck itself, including materials like lumber, Fasteners, and concrete footings, which can range from 20-40 pounds per square foot for a standard western red cedar deck. When calculating joist spacing and beam sizing, you'll typically use a multiplier to account for live load, often called the \"safety factor\" for live loads or the \"0.2 live load factor.\" However, the exact multiplier (0.8 or 0.4) recommended by the International Residential Code (IRC) can be used.",
      "source": "target",
